[Music]  welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilshire host and our guest is  Marsha Seon horn  she's the William K lamb and professor  of anthropology and international  affairs at Yale University and a former  president of the Society for medical  anthropology of the American  Anthropological Association professor in  horn is the author of many award-winning  books including the new Arab man and  cosmopolitan conceptions IVF so germs  and global Dubai today we'll talk with  professor in Hoorn about her new book  America's Arab refugees vulnerability  and health on the margins  welcome professor Ann horn thank you I'm  very pleased to be here yes it's great  to have you on again so let's talk about  your newest book tell us about it  yeah this book is probably the first  anthropological account of the situation  facing Arab refugees who have come into  the United States over the past decades  and it's a book that really explains the  difficulties and why people fled from  the Middle Eastern region because of all  the conflict that's been going on in the  region you know again for decades and  this book is called America's Arab  refugees because in a way my main goal I  think is to show how America's  involvement in conflict in the Middle  Eastern region has in fact led to  overwhelming problems of refugee flight  especially from the countries of Iraq  and Afghanistan and not an Arab country  but a Middle Eastern country the wars  that are really ongoing in those  countries and then obviously the crisis  now in Syria and increasingly in Yemen  so that there really are some serious  conflicts in which America is involved  that have led to the flight of millions  of Arab refugees  and only a very small percentage of them  are getting into the u.s. but they're  here and this book really looks at what  happens to Arab refugees in this country  okay and a lot of your work is done  overseas with this show this book really  is centered in the United States what  led you to pursue this yeah to be honest  I was never really intending to write a  book about Arab refugees in America but  they were a population that I discovered  in a project that took place between an  anthropological study in Lebanon and an  anthropological study in the United Arab  Emirates and during a period of about  five years I ended up going back and  forth to what is probably the largest  Arab ethnic Enclave community in America  which is in the greater metropolitan  Detroit Michigan the city of Detroit and  particularly an area called Dearborn  which scholars have dubbed Arab Detroit  because there are as many as 400,000  maybe even 1/2 million Arabs living in  this part of this part of Detroit and I  was working in a health clinic in  Dearborn and most of the people that I  started seeing and I started talking to  were actually men and women who had fled  from war zones  you know many were Iraqi refugees who  had come either from the first Gulf War  or the war that started in 2003 that the  u.s. started in 2003 or they were from  the southern parts of Lebanon and had  fled during that long conflict that  started in 1975 and Lebanon and lasted  until the year 2000 so they these were  people who had fled they were living in  Michigan and frankly most of them were  not doing that well sort of economically  they were facing a lot of hardship as  refugees in this country so let's talk  about the methodology you used for the  book I know you did a lot of interviews  tell us about that yes so we  anthropologists we have sort of two ways  we call our methodology ethnography  which is really either you know  interviews very in-depth interviews with  people sometimes hour-long  hours long interviews and a lot of what  we call purchase of an observation  hanging about and sort of participating  with people as they're going about their  daily lives and so in this study I  worked with almost a hundred Arab men  and women mostly men actually and they  were all presenting at a clinic in  Dearborn that was really an Arab serving  clinic the physician there was an Arab  spoke Arabic and they were coming in  this case because they had serious  reproductive health problems rather  daunting problems that required a lot of  intervention and a lot of money that  most of them really could ill afford so  I was really looking at how you know in  America our health care system which is  a private health care system where you  have to pay for your health care  services was really not serving this  very vulnerable refugee population  people who were basically impoverished  and something that we forget about is  the level of poverty in a lot of  American big cities Detroit as a city is  considered to be the poorest big city in  America yet it has taken in a  disproportionate share of the arab  refugee population in fact the three  states that have the highest number of  Arab refugees are California then  Michigan and Texas and so a state that  has its own economic problems is in a  way trying to cope with a huge influx of  refugees that are either getting placed  there or who choose to migrate there  because there is a sort of Arab  community to which they can you know  belong mm-hmm so in talking to these men  and some women what were some of the  trends that you saw most of these men  had been through horrific things in the  wars you know some had lived under  Saddam Hussein he had been conscripted  into the army some had spent years in  prison some had been tortured some had  fled their family members had been  killed they fled often with very little  some of the Iraqis had fled through  Saudi Arabia and were placed in refugee  camps in Saudi Arabia before they made  their way to the United States and so  they had been through some grueling  you know and difficult and health  demoting circumstances and the book  really looks at what are the health  costs of conflict what happens to people  physically mentally you know  infrastructurally what happens to  families when they're forced to flee  because of a conflict and really my book  shows all of these detrimental sort of  circumstances that people bring with  them in a way when they come to the  states and then the book really looks at  resettlement in America and how the US  has or has not really put in sufficient  effort into refugee resettlement  interestingly after the Vietnam War  there were hundreds of thousands of  Vietnamese refugees who made their way  to this country and in those days back  in the early 1980s the US government did  do its best to support you know  Vietnamese families they often got three  years of cash assistance they were  helped to get jobs they were helped to  get their lives you know up and running  and over the years the sort of  assistance given to refugees has just  gotten less and less and less now  refugees are lucky if they get eight  months of cash assistance they're told  to get jobs immediately because they  need to be you know self-sufficient  before a years out and so they often end  up in very low paying minimum-wage jobs  they often are scraping by and so they  end up in a situation of real chronic  poverty they never really can get off  the ground often they have skills that  they bring with them some of the people  I worked with who had been engineers  they had been professionals in their  home countries they come to the United  States and they're working as Busboys  and restaurants or you know they're not  working and professional they get no  recertification they often don't have  English because they don't get time to  get English language skills down so it's  a it the term that we used in medical  anthropology is structural vulnerability  these people because of their position  in society coming as refugees no English  low-income you know sometimes poor  educational backgrounds very little  money no social support systems living  in a city that's impoverished they are  very vulnerable and they often there lie  end up being fairly vulnerable as they  go along it wasn't all you know  unfortunate stories there were a few  people in my study who had actually done  surprisingly well and I tried to sort of  showcase the sort of hope and aspiration  and desire for a new life a better life  in America you know you tell the story  about one of them yeah there was a man I  call him Kemal that was not his you know  real name but he was fortunate in that  he had come with his two brothers there  were three he had been in the Army under  Saddam Hussein he had seen horrific  things he had survived and he fled with  his two brothers and they were all  placed together as a sort of group of  three brothers in you know this Detroit  community and together because the three  of them sort of supported each other  they were able to slowly sort of make a  life he ended up eventually owning two  small businesses they bought two small  fixer-upper homes and so they sort of  began achieving the American dream of  home ownership of it being a small  business entrepreneur and he was able to  accrue enough money he had a very  serious male infertility problem which  who knows it could have been related to  the sort of toxic exposures of the Iraq  war but he ended up being able to use  technology it's a variant of in vitro  fertilization that helped him to have a  baby with his wife and it was expensive  I mean to do that technology in America  usually costs more than twelve thousand  US dollars he had his one shot of doing  it and he was very lucky to have a baby  so he was a happy person you know he  told me his story you know showed me the  pictures of his son and he was you know  what I would call a sort of a refugee  success story and I have to say here in  New Haven we have an amazing sort of  community of support for Refugees  through iris our refugee resettlement  agency Yale through the legal services  of Yale the medical services of Yale  Medical School and so there are refugees  especially from Iraq and increasingly  from Afghanistan and Syria who are  coming here and are beginning to sort of  shape their lives and I can name success  stories right here in the Yale system so  refugees can do well but I would our  you in my book that the majority are  vulnerable very structurally vulnerable  and they have a lot of health problems  that are just not being dealt with in  your book could you offer suggestions on  what we can do in this country to  improve the situation of not only Arab  refugees but other refugees yeah I  believe and I think this is maybe my  strongest message of the book is that  America has a moral responsibility to  refugees especially refugees coming from  countries in which America started the  wars that led to the displacement and  the destroyed lives of people and for me  the wars that really matter in this  millennium are the war in Iraq and the  war in Afghanistan and America is also  very much involved from the air at least  in terms of dropping American armaments  on Syria on Yemen on Libya the places  that are war zones in the Middle East  and so if you make a war and you break a  country you have a responsibility for  fixing the country and fixing the lives  of people you know whose lives you've  destroyed and I think others share my  sentiments we have a moral  responsibility to refugees from  countries where we've you know we  started wars and so for me I think the  main story of this book is the Iraqi  refugee story that we never talk about  anymore because you know Iraq the war  was started what in 2003 supposedly it  ended in 2011 but it really hasn't ended  they're still American troops on the  ground in that country there's still  people fleeing that country the three  major groups of refugees coming out of  the Middle East are from Syria Iraq  Afghanistan and certainly they're going  to be Yemenis coming out of the region  too and so I do feel that as a society  we do have a moral responsibility to  help not only in just giving  humanitarian aid but when in bringing  all the people who are seeking asylum in  our country and right now I'll tell you  that the priority if there is priority  being given to refugees it's for men who  have helped the US military in Iraq and  Afghanistan they're given special  immigrant visas called sibs to come to  this country and so they're being  prioritized but there are many other  people from those countries who you know  who need need refuge and somebody needs  to help those families that are stuck in  refugee camps in you know various  countries and so I believe you know some  of the crucial things we need to really  give people a good period of assistance  they need to be housed they need to be  given sustainable jobs and they need to  get English so that they can you know  participate in American society it  should be longer than eight months you  know we need to really restore the kind  of benefits that used to be available  and I also want to say right now in this  year we are at the lowest level of  refugee admissions really in recent  history you know lower than even in 1980  because of the current political regime  in which we're living and this sort of  resistance to bringing immigrants and  refugees into this country although I  must say no refugee has ever committed  an act of terrorism on US soil  no refugee of any kind and so refugees  have been a law-abiding population in  this country and so we can't lump them  in as a sort of you know group of  dangerous people because most of them  have proven that they're not dangerous  and we need to do something to support  those people and that's really what my  book is arguing the kind of conclusion  of my book is a call for sort of justice  if you will for you know Arab refugees  around the world well hopefully that  will come hopefully that will come thank  you for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work Marcia thank  you Marilyn thank you very much for more  information about Professor in Hoorn and  her research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  [Music]  you

